@c ===================================================================
@node Overview
@chapter Overview

@command{rl} is the command-line interface for the rl programming language.
It provides tools for running, checking, compiling, packaging, and generating
documentation for rl source files.

If no command is given and stdin is a terminal, the interactive REPL is started.

@c ===================================================================
@node Commands
@chapter Commands

@table @code
@item rl run @var{file} [@option{-c} @var{code}] [@option{--vm}] [@option{--cranelift}] [@option{--} @var{args}...]
Lex, parse, and evaluate a @file{.rl} source file.

@item rl dev [@option{--vm}] [@option{--cranelift}]
Run the current project by reading @file{rl.toml}.

@item rl new @var{name} [@option{--no-git}] [@option{--script}]
Scaffold a new project directory or create a standalone script.

@item rl check @var{file}
Type-check a @file{.rl} file and report errors without running it.

@item rl format @var{file}
Format a @file{.rl} source file in place.

@item rl compile @var{file} [@option{-o} @var{output}]
Lex, parse, resolve, and compile a @file{.rl} source file to @file{.rlc} bytecode.

@item rl print @var{file} (@option{-t} | @option{-p} | @option{-a})
Print debug information for a @file{.rl} source file.

@item rl transpile @var{file} [@option{-o} @var{output}] [@option{--runtime}] [@option{-c}] [@option{--opt} @var{level}] [@option{--} @var{cc-flags}]
Transpile a @file{.rl} source file to C99.

@item rl package @var{file} [@option{-o} @var{output}] [@option{--vm}]
Package a @file{.rl} file into a self-contained binary.

@item rl repl
Start the interactive TUI REPL.

@item rl lsp
Start the Language Server Protocol server.

@item rl docs @var{topic} [@option{--tui}] [@option{--website}] [@option{--json}] [@option{--stdlib}] [@option{--concept}] [@option{--tutorial}]
Print language reference and stdlib documentation.

@item rl workflows @option{--check} | @option{--package}
Scaffold GitHub Actions workflow files.
@end table

@c ===================================================================
@node run Command
@chapter run Command

@command{rl run @var{file}} lexes, parses, and evaluates a @file{.rl} source
file. Arguments after the file path are passed to the script as its @code{argv}
array.

@section Options

@table @code
@item @option{-c} @var{code}, @option{--code} @var{code}
Execute inline rl code instead of a file.

@item @option{--vm}
Run through the bytecode VM. This is the default backend.

@item @option{--cranelift}
JIT compile via cranelift. Requires the @option{--cranelift} feature.
@end table

@section Examples

@example
$ rl run hello.rl
$ rl run script.rl -- --verbose input.txt
$ rl run -c 'println("hello")'
@end example

@c ===================================================================
@node dev Command
@chapter dev Command

@command{rl dev} runs the current project by reading @file{rl.toml} in the
current directory.

@section Options

@table @code
@item @option{--vm}
Run through the bytecode VM.

@item @option{--cranelift}
JIT compile via cranelift.
@end table

@section Examples

@example
$ rl dev
@end example

@c ===================================================================
@node new Command
@chapter new Command

@command{rl new @var{name}} scaffolds a new project directory with the standard
structure and a @file{rl.toml} configuration file. Alternatively, with the
@option{--script} flag, it creates a single standalone @file{.rl} file.

@section Options

@table @code
@item @option{--no-git}
Skip running @command{git init} in the new project directory.

@item @option{--script}
Create a standalone @file{.rl} script instead of a project directory.
@end table

@section Examples

@example
$ rl new my_project
$ rl new --script my_script
@end example

@c ===================================================================
@node check Command
@chapter check Command

@command{rl check @var{file}} type-checks a @file{.rl} source file and
reports errors without running it.

@section Examples

@example
$ rl check script.rl
@end example

@c ===================================================================
@node format Command
@chapter format Command

@command{rl format @var{file}} formats a @file{.rl} source file in place
according to the rl coding style.

@section Examples

@example
$ rl format script.rl
@end example

@c ===================================================================
@node compile Command
@chapter compile Command

@command{rl compile @var{file}} lexes, parses, resolves, and compiles a
@file{.rl} source file to @file{.rlc} bytecode.

@section Options

@table @code
@item @option{-o} @var{path}, @option{--output} @var{path}
Output @file{.rlc} path. Defaults to @var{file} with its extension changed
to @file{.rlc}.
@end table

@section Examples

@example
$ rl compile script.rl
$ rl compile script.rl -o output.rlc
@end example

@c ===================================================================
@node print Command
@chapter print Command

@command{rl print @var{file}} prints debug information for a @file{.rl}
source file. Exactly one of @option{--tokens}, @option{--parser}, or
@option{--ast} must be given.

@section Options

@table @code
@item @option{-t}, @option{--tokens}
Print the token stream from the lexer.

@item @option{-p}, @option{--parser}
Print the parsed AST (before type checking).

@item @option{-a}, @option{--ast}
Print the type-checked AST.
@end table

@section Examples

@example
$ rl print script.rl --tokens
$ rl print script.rl --parser
$ rl print script.rl --ast
@end example

@c ===================================================================
@node transpile Command
@chapter transpile Command

@command{rl transpile @var{file}} lexes, parses, resolves, type-checks, and
transpiles a @file{.rl} source file to C99 source code.

@section Options

@table @code
@item @option{-o} @var{path}, @option{--output} @var{path}
Output @file{.c} path. Defaults to @var{file} with its extension changed
to @file{.c}.

@item @option{--runtime}
Also emit @file{rl_runtime.h} and @file{rl_runtime.c} alongside the
transpiled output.

@item @option{-c}, @option{--compile}
After transpiling, invoke @command{cc} to compile the @file{.c} file.

@item @option{--opt} @var{level}
Optimization level forwarded to @command{cc}. Accepted values: @code{O0},
@code{O1}, @code{O2}, @code{O3}, @code{Os}, @code{Og}. Defaults to
@code{O2} when @option{--compile} is used.

@item @option{--} @var{cc-flags}
Extra flags forwarded directly to @command{cc}.
@end table

@section Examples

@example
$ rl transpile script.rl
$ rl transpile script.rl --runtime --compile
$ rl transpile script.rl --runtime --compile -o output
$ rl transpile script.rl --runtime --compile -O3 -- -lm
@end example

@c ===================================================================
@node package Command
@chapter package Command

@command{rl package @var{file}} packages a @file{.rl} file into a
self-contained binary. The @file{.rl} source text is embedded directly
into the compiled output.

@section Options

@table @code
@item @option{-o} @var{path}, @option{--output} @var{path}
Output binary path. Defaults to @code{program}.

@item @option{--vm}
Compile to @file{.rlc} bytecode first and embed that instead of raw
source text. This reduces the size of the embedded source at the cost
of needing a @file{.rlc}-aware runtime.
@end table

@section Examples

@example
$ rl package script.rl
$ rl package script.rl -o myapp
$ rl package script.rl --vm
@end example

@c ===================================================================
@node repl Command
@chapter repl Command

@command{rl repl} starts the interactive TUI REPL (Read-Eval-Print Loop)
with syntax highlighting, line editing, and persistent history.

@section Examples

@example
$ rl repl
@end example

@c ===================================================================
@node lsp Command
@chapter lsp Command

@command{rl lsp} starts the Language Server Protocol server over stdio.
Use this with editors that support LSP for autocompletion, diagnostics,
and go-to-definition.

@section Examples

@example
$ rl lsp
@end example

@c ===================================================================
@node docs Command
@chapter docs Command

@command{rl docs @var{topic}} searches names across all documentation
categories and prints the matching entry. With no @var{topic}, it prints
everything. Use the @option{--tui} flag for an interactive terminal browser.

@section Options

@table @code
@item @option{--tui}
Browse docs in an interactive terminal UI.

@item @option{--website}
Open docs in a web browser.

@item @option{--json}
Print output as JSON instead of Markdown.

@item @option{--stdlib}
Restrict search to stdlib docs.

@item @option{--concept}
Restrict search to concept docs.

@item @option{--tutorial}
Restrict search to tutorial docs.

@item @option{--out-file} @var{path}
Write output to a file instead of stdout.

@item @option{--generate}
Generate documentation for the current project.

@item @option{--html}
Emit an HTML version of the generated site (used with @option{--generate}).

@item @option{--out-dir} @var{path}
Output directory for @option{--generate}.
@end table

@section Examples

@example
$ rl docs
$ rl docs map
$ rl docs --tui
$ rl docs --stdlib --json
$ rl docs --generate --html --out-dir site/
@end example

@c ===================================================================
@node workflows Command
@chapter workflows Command

@command{rl workflows} scaffolds GitHub Actions workflow files. At least
one of @option{--check} or @option{--package} must be given.

@section Options

@table @code
@item @option{--check}
Generate a workflow that runs @command{rl check} on push/PR.

@item @option{--package}
Generate a workflow that packages and releases a binary.
@end table

@section Examples

@example
$ rl workflows --check
$ rl workflows --package
$ rl workflows --check --package
@end example

@c ===================================================================
@node Examples
@chapter Examples

@section Running a Script

@example
$ rl run hello.rl
Hello, world!
@end example

@section Running with Arguments

@example
$ rl run script.rl -- --verbose input.txt
@end example

@section Inline Code

@example
$ rl run -c 'println("hello")'
hello
@end example

@section Type Checking

@example
$ rl check script.rl
@end example

@section Formatting

@example
$ rl format script.rl
@end example

@section Compiling to Bytecode

@example
$ rl compile script.rl -o output.rlc
@end example

@section Printing Tokens

@example
$ rl print script.rl --tokens
@end example

@section Transpiling to C

@example
$ rl transpile script.rl --runtime --compile
@end example

@section Packaging a Binary

@example
$ rl package script.rl -o myapp
@end example

@section Starting the REPL

@example
$ rl repl
repl> 2 + 2
4
repl> exit
@end example

